it's great to be joined today by dr.  Joseph saccharin who is number one a  former victim of gun violence also a  clinician and researcher at Johns  Hopkins Hospital and founder of the  hashtag this is our lane social media  movement with the goal of reducing  firearm death and disability in America  dr. saccharin great to talk to you first  of all yeah thanks so much for having me  David so let's talk first about how the  sort of controversy started involving  doctors who work on treating and  reducing gun violence in the United  States and the NRA the hashtag this is  our lane if people haven't been  following this controversy what's it all  about yeah so the night before Thousand  Oaks  mass shooting November 7th I believe the  NRA sent out a communication and a  message that essentially implied that  the medical community should stay in  their Lane when it comes to providing  solutions around gun violence in America  and in fact not only did they say that  but they also said that we had pretty  much not consulted anyone else and so  you know those you know types of  rhetoric and communication is what  really started this movement and United  healthcare professionals all across this  country your particular background is  relevant here because you were the  victim of gun violence basically by  being in the wrong place at the wrong  time can you talk about that a little  bit yeah so at the age of 17 I was  nearly killed after being shot in the  throat and you know David at that point  in my life I really had no idea what I  wanted to do in the future you know  you're young you don't realize the fact  that you're mortal but I was inspired I  was really  inspired to take that second chance that  I was given and really provide other  people with the same types of  opportunities and so and that's what  drove me into medicine that's what drove  me to becoming a trauma surgeon and then  really you know for many years I've been  working at that intersection of medicine  public health and public policy to try  to make communities safe for all across  America we've seen attempts in politics  in general to sort of try to discredit  groups with certain characteristics as  being legitimate sort of authorities or  legitimately enabled to comment on  particular issues so like without having  any idea what your politics are some  some ideas that that I present on my  show include you know on on climate  science we've seen the so called climate  skeptic movement try to say well a lot  of those scientists don't practice the  exact right type of science that they  would really know about climate change  or we saw during the LGBTQ rights debate  some who opposed LGBT rights say well if  you're part of the LGBT community you  shouldn't have a say because you're  biased of course ignoring that not being  in a group provides the same type of  bias that being in a group just from a  different perspective so can you talk a  little bit here about why folks in your  position and the medical profession  actually are in a position to talk about  gun violence in the United States yeah  well I think it's a really important  question and in fact there's there's a  couple really I think critical pieces in  your question the first is what is going  on in this country surrounding gun  violence is really an attempt to  polarize a topic it's an attempt to  continue this false narrative that as  Americans we are so divided when it  comes to this issue and I think David  when you look at the leadership of the  gun lobby and the membership there  a significant disparity that exists yes  and in fact I've talked to hundreds if  not thousands of gun owners and guess  what we pretty much agree on most of the  aspects and solutions that need to be  taken in order to move the needle  forward on this the second part of your  question I think relates to us as  clinicians and I'll just say this you  know we are front and center of taking  care of these patients day in and day  out and so for a group or an individual  to think that health care professionals  are not part of the solution I think  shows a real lack of understanding of  how complex this problem is and the  reality is is that there is no one  individual or one organization that's  going to solve this problem this is  going to require stakeholders from  diverse backgrounds and views you know  including healthcare gun owners policy  makers and so forth one of the positions  that we heard from individuals  associated with the NRA if not the NRA  itself is that doctors should just treat  people and basically otherwise shut up  but even without having to address that  in this particular case like as a  general rule isn't it very common that  doctors work both in treatment but also  research and prevention absolutely I  mean first of all you know this is farm  injury and death in this country is a  public health crisis and you know part  of our role is to treat this not just  when they come into our trauma centers  but before they ever get shot the injury  prevention piece that you talk about and  so that is something when you look at  other public health problems we have  dealt with time and time again for  example motor vehicle fatalities you  know in the 60s and 70s we were having  high fatality --zz we didn't get rid of  cars  you know we made seatbelts and airbags  and made the road safer so it's that  type of approach that's gonna be  required in order for us to really see  some change and reduce injury all across  this country yeah it seems like the the  gun rights groups want to argue that you  getting involved not only in treatment  but also prevention is like a special  pleading that in this one case doctors  want to get involved in prevention but  not in others it actually seems like the  special pleading would be asking doctors  to ignore the prevention piece when it  comes to gun gun violence when with all  other types of disease and public health  issues doctors are the ones that are  right in there working on that out angle  yeah that's correct I mean imagine if a  patient got brought into my trauma Bay  today and I decided I was not going to  treat that person right now it's just  completely unacceptable the same way  that it's unacceptable that we don't  work together from an injury prevention  perspective and look the reality is is  that when you look at organizations for  example the American College of Surgeons  they actually reached out to the NRA in  2017 to figure out how can we  collaborate and work together in order  to reduce firemen during death so you  know that second part of their statement  where we essentially consulted no one is  categorically false and again goes to  spreading this false narrative and I  think a lot of the leadership wants out  there I'd love to talk a little bit  about the research angle there is  something called the Dickey amendment  from 1996 which prevents federally  funded research from supporting gun  control or gun safety measures can you  talk a little bit about the ways that  doctors can get around that aside from  just sticking to private studies like  are there ways to circumvent that to  keep doing good work on this issue yeah  so what we've what we've seen over the  past 20 years essentially is you know  this you know end to really federally  funding  gun bans research and when you look at  the burden of disease that exists it's  just tremendous and it's nowhere near  funded at the level that is required so  right now we're starting to see a couple  things a lot of different private public  partnerships you know for example the  Laura and John normal Arnold Foundation  have committed you know twenty million  dollars with the plan to raise thirty  more million for gun violence research  we saw Blake mikowski who committed five  million dollars towards gun violence  research people are not willing to just  sit on the sidelines and wait for you  know essentially the government to take  action on this I I think the other  important piece is we're also seeing a  lot of great work being done by the gun  violence prevention community so you  know you take a look at the Brady  Campaign which in full disclosure I'm on  the board but they have a great campaign  on n family fire which is really geared  toward safe storage and you look at you  know what Giffords put out today with  encouraging Congress to fund the CDC so  there's a lot of stuff that's going on  and a lot of it is happening in parallel  but we have to think about those private  public partnerships as well what are the  biggest roadblocks right now to getting  anything done because I and my audience  a lot of my audience share this sort of  desperate skepticism that I mean if even  after Sandy Hook close to ninety percent  agreement in the u.s. that every firearm  transaction should come with a  background check universal background  checks and President Obama in in the  White House and seemingly almost every  factor in the right position still  universal background checks could not be  passed  so do you share that skepticism and what  what are the roadblocks other than just  politics well look is it is it  frustrating absolutely but I think it's  also David important to remember that in  this country most governing actually  happens at the local level and so when  you look at communities all across this  country there's a lot of different  you know pieces of legislation that's  been passed and so I say that only to  mention to you know all of your viewers  and Americans all across this country is  that each and every one of us have a  role and responsibility to play in  trying to make our community safer and  that starts within our own communities  but then also has a parallel kind of  effort at the federal level interesting  so so in so I think that that actually  is important because I don't think a lot  of people consider gun safety  regulations local issues beyond just  ordinances allowing businesses to say we  don't allow firearms here but so you're  suggesting that an approach would be to  try to tackle some of these issues  would it be at the municipal level at  the state level well I think it's both  and we start to see it Pittsburgh just  passed recently some some firearm  legislation you know we see different  states with you know some relatively  strict legislation that exists and a lot  of this has happened honestly because  there's been significant grassroots  movements when you look at you know  groups like you know moms demand and a  variety of other you know gun violence  prevention organizations like Brady and  Giffords they're all working at the  state level in the community level in  addition to the federal stuff and  sometimes that doesn't get highlighted  but it's also David one of the reasons  that voter turnout rate in our country  is so low because most people don't feel  like they can make a difference yes I  think we have to change that that  culture and that thought process  absolutely and that that can sometimes  be an uphill battles as I've experienced  we've been speaking with dr. Joseph  saccharin clinician researcher at the  johns hopkins hospital also founder of  the this is our lane social media  movement thank you so much for talking  to me today thank you so much have a  great day

